Emacsvox News -- History Of User-Visible Changes

Maintained by Emacsvox Contributors.

* Emacsvox Unreleased --- Current User-Visible Changes

Released notes are preserved in [[file:NEWS-2026.9.5][NEWS for Emacsvox 2026.9.5]].

** Advance past partly visible text without repeating it

In short graphical windows, Down no longer consumes a press by scrolling
ordinary text without moving the cursor and then reading that line again.
This fixes repeated lines observed in Notmuch messages. Rows taller than the
window retain native partial scrolling; scrolling preferences are unchanged.

** Keep EAT observation working at the right margin

Terminal output ending exactly at the right margin no longer raises a cursor
position error or interrupts screen observation.

** Speak EAT output reliably while it scrolls

Large bursts and repeated lines now produce bounded speech even after earlier
rows leave the screen or scrollback is trimmed. Split chunks and wrapped lines
are assembled without repeating command input. Automatic output remains limited
to eight lines and 1,000 characters, with an indication of omitted output.

** Control EAT foreground feedback with C-e C-q

Each terminal now has an independent automatic-feedback toggle. Output requires
Emacs keyboard focus when detectable, and remains available while editing a
line-mode draft. Losing focus or switching feedback off discards locally pending
work; speech already sent may finish. Explicit reading, input feedback, verbosity
and background monitoring remain independent.

** Recognize EAT prompts without hiding interactive questions

Ready prompts identified by EAT shell integration now give a cue by default.
Set =emacsvox-eat-prompt-feedback= to =text= or =silent= to change that behavior;
=C-e q p= explicitly reads the last identified prompt. Split and multiline
prompts are bounded and redacted. Unmarked remote prompts, password requests,
and confirmation questions remain ordinary output and speak without a newline.

** Keep EAT terminal bells free of spoken labels

Terminal bells retain their configured sound without speaking "Terminal bell".
The semantic =command-terminal-bell= event lets an aural rule add a spoken
label when wanted.

** Describe Agent Shell's merged queue submissions

When Agent Shell submits several queued prompts together, Emacsvox announces
the number started and the number remaining.

** Disambiguate Agent Shell's grouped settings

Confirmed settings with duplicate names include their group, matching Agent
Shell's completion choices.

** Speak symbol names cleanly during character navigation

Names such as "left bracket" and "question mark" no longer speak an extra
asterisk or bracket names. This correction works at every punctuation level
and does not require an Omnivox update.

** Create named punctuation profiles

In =omnivox-punctuation=, =N= creates a profile inheriting a built-in level;
edit its character pronunciations, Save, then restart explicitly. Select it with
=tts-set-punctuations= or a mode policy. Both workers must report matching
profiles; older or differing servers use the saved built-in fallback.

** Diagnose each speech worker separately

Aural Doctor reports main and notification capabilities, engine availability,
acknowledged configuration agreement, and configured log locations without
starting or probing speech. Press =y= to copy the displayed report.

** Edit engine configuration without restarting speech

Open /Engine configuration/ from engine details, or run
=omnivox-engine-configuration=. Edit independent drafts, register installed
helpers disabled, save safely, and explicitly Apply saved engine and voice
settings to both speech workers. Requires the updated local Omnivox service.

** Read tabulated application lists with shared table controls

Control-Meta arrows or =C-e C-t C-r= enter explicit shared reading in ordinary
tabulated lists. Read full rows and cells, including truncated values and blank
fields, while retaining application actions. Refresh ends reading, and =q=
restores ordinary navigation. EWW, table.el and SES are unchanged.

** Read the manual as plain text

Run =make docs-org-text= to export the complete manual as UTF-8 text under
=.docs-preview/org-manual/emacsvox.txt=. Keys, literals and references now
use ordinary Org markup, so standard Org text and HTML exports retain them.
The maintained Info export preserves keyboard formatting and existing nodes.
Thanks to Lubos Pintes for the plain-text export contribution.

** Use the palette voice for character feedback

Typing and character navigation now use the palette's default voice, including
physical choices such as eSpeak Max and their fallback tuning. Letter feedback
retains character speed and uppercase pitch. This requires an updated Omnivox
server advertising palette character support; older servers keep their legacy
letter behavior. Punctuation names now also use normal palette speech.

** Replay the voice that delivered a notification

Original playback in Recent Feedback and Feedback Details now uses the engine
and physical voice reported during delivery, including fallback choices, even
after changing palettes. This requires a new, completed recording with complete
voice evidence. Older or incomplete recordings report that original playback
is unavailable. Resetting the notification server also restores its Omnivox
configuration and default voice.

** Hear apostrophes when typing or navigating in Org

Character echo and character navigation now identify letters and numbers by
Unicode category instead of the major mode's word syntax. Straight apostrophes
in Org are announced as "apostrophe", independently of the prose punctuation
level. Letter and digit feedback still respects the existing Unicode settings.
Thanks to Joe Luke for the report and reproduction.

** Review and edit punctuation pronunciations

Open =M-x omnivox-punctuation= or /Punctuation/ in Aural Home's /Voices and
speech/ group. Review host-provided defaults, edit each existing punctuation
level, preserve a character or restore its default. Drafts survive leaving the
screen. Save validates on the local speech host and preserves other settings;
restart both speech workers separately when ready. File conflicts retain the
draft. This requires an updated local Omnivox host with punctuation editing.

** Apply a physical voice across a palette

Press =V= on a physical voice in Browse voices or on an eSpeak variant to
review it across all named voices, including =default=. Update a personal
palette or create a new independent copy. Existing matching choices are
promoted with their tuning; shared adjustments and remaining fallbacks are
preserved. Compare original and proposed voices before one coordinated save,
with separate Save and apply and Save to collection actions and retry support.

** Edit spoken labels from Feedback Details

Spoken Before and After labels, including =indent 2=, now have individual
playback controls and links to their named palette voices. Open a field in
Feedback Details and select Edit named voice indent to tune its voice using
the recorded label as the preview text. On a label, =O= plays only that label;
=C= or =r= opens its named voice editor. Original recordings remain unchanged.

** Default reading voice in palettes

Each voice palette now includes =default= for ordinary reading. In eSpeak's
Voice variants browser, select Max, press =u=, and choose your palette and
=default=. Preview it and use Save and apply in the existing editor. The
choice, fallbacks and tuning belong to that palette and follow palette
switches on both speech streams. Other named voices keep their own settings.
Use at Startup remains the separate action for choosing a startup palette.

** Interruptible Notmuch refresh and EAT history feedback

Refreshing the selected Notmuch search buffer queues its completion count on
the primary speech stream. Navigation interrupts it, and it no longer speaks
over the selected message through the notification stream. Refreshes that
finish in another buffer retain generic notification feedback.

Opening a Notmuch thread now selects its newest open, matching unread
message. When none is unread, it keeps Notmuch's initial selection.
Reading a sender line after a folded quote no longer goes silent when Emacs
leaves point in the preceding hidden text.

EAT line-mode history navigation speaks the recalled command without also
announcing the history-item counter or input-restored message. Those messages
remain visible in the echo area; unrelated messages and errors are preserved.

** Native Windows installation

The native Windows x64 installer can download a verified complete GNU Emacs
with =-DownloadEmacs=. Use =-Offline= with a prepared archive cache to install
without network access. Existing explicit Emacs selections are preserved.

Development Windows Setup builds add a per-user wizard, Start menu and
optional desktop shortcuts, repair and uninstall. Uninstall preserves the
user's profile, logs and downloaded voices by default, with separate optional
cleanup choices and an explanation of any retained shared voices. The wizard
names the bundled versions, uses Emacsvox Windows for shortcuts, and explains
launch and uninstall when installation finishes.

** Welcome screen

An idle first startup offers speech testing, rate and output settings, voices,
the tutorial and manual. The startup toggle is saved per Emacs profile.
Reopen the screen with C-h W or M-x emacsvox-welcome.

** Agent Shell

Agent Shell 0.78.1 support keeps brackets editable at the persistent prompt
while the agent works. Viewport submission feedback distinguishes queueing
from steering, including the override command and unsupported-steering
fallback. Full header speech includes the optional cumulative cost indicator.
Reading a busy live prompt briefly says "Agent busy" or "Waiting for
permission", without repeating how input will be submitted.

Rendered table borders are omitted from response and line speech, preventing
decorative box-drawing characters from forcing an Eloquence-to-eSpeak switch.
Literal box-drawing characters now receive Unicode names through the existing
emoji and symbol naming settings.
Control-Meta-Left and Control-Meta-Right move between table columns; plain Left
and Right retain character navigation.
Outside editable input, t and T jump to the next and previous rendered table.
Both keys insert normally in live prompts and the viewport editor.
